WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
Containers provide operating-system-level process isolation.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:09.696
Unlike a full virtual machine, a typical container does not bring a separate guest kernel.

00:00:09.696 --> 00:00:18.760
Its processes rely on the host kernel while runtime and kernel controls limit what those processes can see and do.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:29.510
Container escape is the failure or abuse of those isolation assumptions so activity crosses from a container toward the host or a broader platform.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:34.358
The phrase describes a security outcome, not one specific technique.

00:00:34.358 --> 00:00:43.000
The boundary may be weakened by configuration, excessive privilege, exposed host interfaces, vulnerable platform components, or several conditions combined.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.691
Escape risk matters because cloud-native workloads are densely scheduled and highly automated.

00:00:48.691 --> 00:00:57.334
A host may run several applications, hold node credentials, connect to orchestration services, and access shared storage or networks.

00:00:57.334 --> 00:01:02.603
Crossing one boundary can therefore expose assets beyond the original workload.

00:01:02.603 --> 00:01:13.353
Threat modeling asks which boundary should contain a compromised process, what privileged interfaces are reachable, and how far impact could spread if containment fails.

00:01:13.353 --> 00:01:18.623
It also identifies which preventive and detective controls support each assumption.

00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:26.001
The model should not assume that containers are unsafe by definition or perfectly secure by default.

00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:35.486
Containers can provide strong practical isolation when workloads, runtimes, nodes, identities, and orchestration policy are designed and maintained as a system.

00:01:35.486 --> 00:01:38.648
This course stays defensive and conceptual.

00:01:38.648 --> 00:01:46.025
The objective is to prevent boundary failure, reduce blast radius, detect suspicious interaction, and prepare recovery.

00:01:46.025 --> 00:01:52.138
Secure design makes a workload compromise harder to expand and easier to contain.
